 care homes  will go bust and there will still be  thousands upon thousands of people in  this country who don't get access to the  social care they need so i'm afraid i  gently and politely disagree with the  way in which you frame this okay um i've  allowed you to explain at length why you  think the government's wrong and i hope  that we've dealt with that point now  because i really want to talk about what  labour actually thinks  about some practical things  as well as some  broader political questions um first of  all are you going to go along with the  government's uh  likely decision to end the need for pcr  tests after travel uh if that's what  they decide to do so the health  secretaries seem to suggest uh earlier  on  well we'll  we'll have to see what the proposal is  when it comes before parliament we've  had some briefing into the newspapers  today  that certain elements of the coronavirus  act will be taken off the statute book  at first sight based on the briefing the  clauses which are going to be taken off  the statute book that looks like a  reasonable approach to me but obviously  we'll want to study the detail when it  comes to parliament because there have  been huge concerns about the way in  which that the coronavirus act has been  uh misused by the authorities and  ridiculous fines have been imposed on  people when they've been you know  sitting on park benches and so on that's  not what this legislation was aimed at  but obviously we'll want to study the  detail we can't just go off what we've  what has been briefed into sunday  newspapers because we want to take we'll  want to take a considered choice in the  round all right well you're you're open  to it okay let's talk about what labour  would do in relation to social care and  you've said  already we don't need to go back over  the tory proposals and all of that um  andy burnham your labour colleague wrote  in the newspaper this week my advice to  my party would be not to leave it too  